# Scope

This acceptance exercises the production `RuntimeCompactionBinding` and
`RuntimeCompactionControl` against a temporary Codex transcript and temporary
queue, status-history, and cursor repositories. It does not launch a native
CLI, contact a provider, access an Ara host, or use the live router state
paths. Provider-budget callbacks are minimal fixture ports; binding,
transcript discovery, checkpoint scanning, durable admission state, terminal
admission projection, and recovery status are production implementations.

# Evidence

* [acceptance.py](acceptance.py) is the exact isolated runner.
* [result.json](result.json) is the payload-free machine-readable result.
* [adjacent-acceptance-tests.txt](adjacent-acceptance-tests.txt) records the
  current exact-file isolated adjacent run: 404 tests, 52.201 seconds,
  successful with 42 skips. It uses the renamed
  `test_runtime_transcript_large_identity.py`, not the obsolete filename.
* The run passed 13 case projections in 0.978591 seconds and deleted its temporary
  fixture directory.
* The main case used a Codex `session_meta` first record over 64 KiB, a
  compacted checkpoint over 600 KiB, over 600 KiB of later ordinary history,
  and a later native `token_count` input of 120. It changed durable admission
  from `blocked` to `verified`, with `next_raw_tokens=120`; terminal admission
  became ready and recovery status reported no reset.
* Reconstructing the binding from the same temporary state restored the same
  epoch and verified state. Persisted `awaiting_shrink` reconstructed and
  verified as well.
* Incomplete JSON, wrong thread identity, pre-request checkpoint timestamps,
  replaced-file wrong header identity, unknown capacity, above-capacity
  usage, and usage not below the 1200-token baseline remained paused. Queue,
  status history, and cursor file SHA-256 digests were unchanged in every
  observation.

